---
import { links } from '../links';
---

<section class="band" id="problem">
  <div class="wrap split reveal">
    <div>
      <h2>Every validator does everything.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Most layer 1 blockchains make you pick two of throughput, decentralization, and
        state. Ethereum keeps decentralization, and the state stays one object every
        validator has to verify, which caps what the base layer carries. Solana pushes that
        ceiling by making the node bigger, which raises the hardware bar for taking part.
        Sui parallelizes execution with an object model and still has every validator
        process every transaction.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual way out is to trust fewer validators or push the work to a layer 2.
        BluePods does neither. Each object has a defined set of <strong>holder
        validators</strong> that store it, attest to it, and execute the transactions that
        touch it. A write to one of an application's objects involves only the ten to fifty
        holders of that object, not the entire network.
      </p>
      <p>
        State and execution split across the network. One order runs underneath all of it.
      </p>
      <a class="jump" href={links.vision} rel="noopener">Read the vision &#8627;</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .prose .jump {
    justify-self: start;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }
</style>
